

{"id":"097ba50a-da50-47d6-b6e0-2f8fca34fbc6","object":"chat.completion","created":1786447183,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_055\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 10,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_03\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 17,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task with an instance and an intervention. The goal is to produce a repair solution: a list of decisions for each mission, with UAV assignment, held flag, path, depart slot, charge blocks. The contract: \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\" means we must keep the same UAV assignment as in the baseline? Actually, the baseline has a set of decisions with assignments. But we are repairing. The assignment_order_contract says \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". That implies we must keep the same UAV for each mission as originally assigned? Or we must keep the same order of missions per UAV? The baseline decisions: \nMISSION_01: UAV_01\nMISSION_02: UAV_02\nMISSION_03: UAV_01\nMISSION_04: UAV_02\nMISSION_05: UAV_02\n\nThus per UAV: UAV_01 gets MISSION_01 then MISSION_03 (order 0 and 1). UAV_02 gets MISSION_02 (seq 1), MISSION_04 (seq 0), MISSION_05 (seq 2). Wait, sequence_index: MISSION_01 seq 0, MISSION_02 seq 1, MISSION_03 seq 1, MISSION_04 seq 0, MISSION_05 seq 2. It's a bit confusing: For each UAV, the missions have sequence indices indicating order. For UAV_01: seq 0: MISSION_01, seq 1: MISSION_03. For UAV_02: seq 0: MISSION_04, seq 1: MISSION_02, seq 2: MISSION_05. So the order is: For UAV_01, first MISSION_01 then MISSION_03. For UAV_02, first MISSION_04, then MISSION_02, then MISSION_05. The contract says \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\", so we must keep exactly these assignments and this per-UAV sequence. We cannot reassign missions to different UAVs, nor change the order. However, we can hold missions (set held=true). If held, path and depart_slot null and charge_blocks 0. So we can choose to hold any mission, which might improve feasibility.\n\nWe need to respect all constraints: route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and interventions.\n\nInterventions: initial_soc_overrides for UAV_01: 1.657522 kWh (replacing the initial 2.735944? Yes, override). Pad capacity override: VP_04 at slot 6 capacity 1 (default 2). Closed corridor slots: COR_09 closed from slot 9-32; COR_07 closed from slot 6-29. So many closures.\n\nWe need to produce valid repair. We'll check each mission and find feasible path, depart slot, charge_blocks, ensuring energy constraints.\n\nLet's start with UAV_01:\nUAV_01: initial_soc: 1.657522 kWh (after override). Battery capacity: 16.0 kWh. Reserve: 1.5 kWh. Charge per block: 1.2 kWh. Start vertiport: VP_04. Available slot: 6 (maybe earliest it can start? Actually available_slot 6 means it's ready from slot 6).\nMissions assigned to UAV_01:\nMission 01: origin VP_04, destination VP_02. Release slot 6, due slot 8. Priority: urgent. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_03, travel 2 slots, energy 0.1017 kWh), PATH_08 (COR_07, travel 2 slots, energy 0.1356 kWh), PATH_09 (COR_02, travel 3 slots, energy 0.113 kWh). PATH_08 uses COR_07 which is closed from slot 6-29 (closed at slots 6,...). So if depart slot 6, COR_07 closed; if depart 7? But length 2, would need corridor during slots of traversal. Need to check closure exactly: closed_corridor_slots lists specific (corridor_id, slot). So corridor is closed at those exact slots. The mission edges have \"offset_slot\" 0, and duration_slots. So if depart at slot d, the edge occupies slots d+0 to d+duration_slots-1 (since offset 0). So for COR_07, if depart slot 6, it occupies slots 6 and 7; both slots 6 and 7 are in closed list (slot 6,7). So not allowed. Same for depart 7: slots 7,8 both closed. So PATH_08 is infeasible for depart slots 6-? Actually, closure list includes slots 6 through 29 for COR_07. So any departure before 29 would have at least one slot in closure. Can depart at 29? Then slots 29 and 30; 29 closed, so no. Depart 30? Horizon slots 30, max? Horizon slots 30. Depart slot cannot be >30? Actually due window 8, must leave after release 6 and finish by due 8. Depart slot must be such that arrival slot <= due? Usually due slot is latest arrival. For Mission 01, due 8. Travel slots: if travel 2, depart slot <=6? But release 6, so only possible depart 6. If travel 3, depart 5? But release 6, depart must >= release. So PATH_09 with travel 3: if depart 6, arrival at 9 (since slots: 6,7,8 travel = 3 slots? Actually travel_slots=3, so depart 6, occupies slots 6,7,8, arrival at end of slot 8 => slot 9? Typically arrival slot = depart + travel_slots, so for 3 slots, if depart 6, arrival 9. But due 8, so late. So PATH_09 not possible due to due. So only PATH_02 (COR_03) with travel 2, depart 6 arrival 8, meeting due exactly. COR_03 not closed. So Mission 01 feasible: path PATH_02, depart 6.\n\nNow, mission 03 for UAV_01: origin VP_02 (after Mission 01 arrives at VP_02 at slot 8). It needs turnaround 1 slot. So after arrival at VP_02, UAV_01 will be free at slot 9 (arrival 8, then 1 slot turnaround, so earliest depart slot 9). Mission 03: origin VP_02, destination VP_05. Release slot 9, due slot 24. Path only PATH_05 (COR_08, travel 1 slot, energy 0.100344 kWh). COR_08 not closed. So depart slot 9, arrival 10. This works.\n\nNow we must handle charging. UAV_01 can add charge_blocks after Mission 01 before Mission 03? The normative SOC event order: after service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. That means the charge_blocks attached to a mission occur at its destination after its turnaround. For Mission 01, we can set charge_blocks up to max 3. UAV_01 starts with 1.657522 kWh. Energy for Mission 01: PATH_02 energy 0.1017 kWh. So after service flight, SOC = initial - 0.1017 = 1.555822 kWh. Reserve check: must have at least 1.5 kWh. 1.555822 > 1.5, so ok. Then turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge. We can charge 0,1,2,3 blocks. Each block adds 1.2 kWh, but capacity 16 kWh. So after charging, SOC = 1.555822 + charge_blocks * 1.2, capped at 16.0. Then this energy is available for next mission (Mission 03). So we need enough energy for Mission 03: PATH_05 energy 0.100344. After Mission 03 service, we must have at least 1.5 reserve. So before Mission 03, SOC must be ≥ 0.100344 + 1.5 = 1.600344. After Mission 01 without charge, we have 1.555822, which is slightly below. So we need at least 1 charge block to get 1.555822+1.2=2.755822 > 1.600344. So charge_blocks for Mission 01 must be at least 1. Can be 1,2,3. No further missions for UAV_01 after Mission 03, so after Mission 03's post-service charge doesn't matter, but we must satisfy reserve: after Mission 03, before turnaround, check reserve. So Mission 03's service energy is deducted, then check reserve. Starting SOC (after charging from Mission 01) = 2.755822 (if 1 block). After service flight of Mission 03: 2.755822 - 0.100344 = 2.655478, reserve check: ≥1.5. Then turnaround and post-service charge (0 blocks perhaps). So that's fine. So we can set Mission 01 charge_blocks=1.\n\nNow UAV_02: initial_soc 10.66308 kWh, battery capacity 22.0 kWh, reserve 2.5 kWh, charge per block 0.95 kWh. Start VP_04. Available slot 6. Sequence for UAV_02: Mission_04 (seq 0) then Mission_02 (seq 1) then Mission_05 (seq 2). Order: first Mission_04, then Mission_02, then Mission_05. So need to check feasibility and scheduling.\n\nMission_04: origin VP_04, destination VP_02. Release slot 6, due slot 29 (generous). Path options: PATH_06 (COR_06, travel 2 slots, energy 0.16308). COR_06 not closed. Could also other paths? Only PATH_06 listed. So depart slot: earliest is 6 (available slot 6, release 6). But pad capacity at VP_04 at slot 6 is overridden to 1 (capacity 1). UAV_01 also departs VP_04 at slot 6? UAV_01 departs at 6 using pad? Yes, Mission 01 departs VP_04 at 6. So two departures at slot 6 from VP_04 would exceed pad capacity (1). So if both depart at same slot, conflict. Need to stagger. UAV_01 must depart at 6 to make due for Mission 01 (urgent). So UAV_02's Mission_04 might need to depart later, e.g., slot 7 or later. Check release 6, so 7 is ok. But then Mission_04 occupies travel slots 7,8 (if depart 7, arrival 9). Due 29, fine. So we can delay Mission_04 to avoid pad conflict. However, also corridor capacities? COR_06 not closed. Pad capacity VP_04 capacity is overridden only at slot 6? Check: intervention: pad_capacity_overrides: [{\"capacity\":1,\"slot\":6,\"vertiport_id\":\"VP_04\"}]. So only slot 6 has capacity 1, other slots default 2. So if Mission_04 departs at 7, pad VP_04 slot 7 has capacity 2, so no conflict if only one departure at 7. UAV_01 departs at 6, so safe. So we can set Mission_04 depart slot 7.\n\nAfter Mission_04, arrival VP_02 at slot 9 (depart 7 + 2 travel = arrival 9). Then turnaround 1 slot, earliest next depart slot 10. Then Mission_02: origin VP_02? Actually Mission_02 origin VP_02, yes. But Mission_02 release slot 9, due 12. So to depart at 10, travel time? Path options: PATH_01 (COR_05, travel 4 slots, energy 0.302), PATH_04 (COR_04, travel 1 slot, wind level 1, energy 0.160815), PATH_07 (COR_09, travel 2 slots, energy 0.151). But COR_09 is closed from slot 9-32, so PATH_07 infeasible because depart 10->slots 10,11 both closed. PATH_01: travel 4 slots, depart 10 -> arrival 14 (due 12, late). PATH_04: travel 1 slot, depart 10 -> arrival 11 (due 12, ok). So PATH_04 is the only feasible path to meet due 12. Check wind: PATH_04 has wind level 1, but UAV_02 max_wind_level 2, so compatible. So Mission_02 feasible with PATH_04 depart 10 arrival 11.\n\nAfter Mission_02, destination VP_01. Turnaround 1 slot, free at slot 12. Then Mission_05: origin VP_01, release slot 17, due slot 26. So UAV_02 must wait at VP_01 until release 17. It can do post-service charge after Mission_02 (and also after Mission_04 maybe). Let's check energy constraints.\n\nUAV_02 energy flow:\nStart: 10.66308\nMission_04: energy 0.16308. So after service flight: 10.66308 - 0.16308 = 10.5 exactly? 10.66308 - 0.16308 = 10.5. Reserve check: 10.5 >= 2.5, ok. Then turnaround (1 slot), then post-service charge at VP_02. We can set charge_blocks for Mission_04. Then after charging, available for Mission_02.\nThen Mission_02: PATH_04 energy 0.160815. After Mission_02 service, SOC = (SOC after charge) - 0.160815. Reserve check: must be >= 2.5. Then turnaround at VP_01, then post-service charge for Mission_02. Then wait until slot 17 for Mission_05 release. During waiting, it's sitting at VP_01; we can also charge post-service after Mission_02. But note charging takes slots: each charge block takes 1 slot? Actually charging occurs as \"post-service charge\", it occupies slots after turnaround. Usually charge_blocks means number of charging slots consumed at the destination charger. Since charger capacity applies to each slot. So we need to ensure charger capacity not exceeded.\n\nAfter Mission_05: energy check: Mission_05 PATH_03 energy 0.2171. Reserve check after service. So we need enough energy.\n\nFirst, determine if any charge needed. If we didn't charge after Mission_04, SOC after Mission_04 = 10.5. Then Mission_02 consumes 0.160815, after service: 10.339185. Reserve check: ok >=2.5. Then Mission_05 needs 0.2171, after service: 10.339185 - 0.2171 = 10.122085, reserve ok. So from energy standpoint, no charging is necessary. However, we might want to charge to improve objective? Lexicographic objective: \"urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" Urgent holds: number of urgent missions held. We have urgent missions: MISSION_01 and MISSION_02. Neither held, so 0. Total holds: sum of helds across all. So 0. Repair distance: presumably sum of distances of chosen paths? Or difference from baseline? Not clear, but we want to minimize something. Weighted tardiness: due dates. delay plus charge wait: maybe sum of (depart - release) plus waiting for charge? energy: total energy used? We'll try to minimize energy and tardiness. Since no charging needed, we can set charge_blocks=0 for all. But check reserve after Mission_05: 10.122085 > 2.5, fine. However, we must also consider charging constraints: if we set 0 charge blocks, then no charger occupancy.\n\nBut wait, we need to verify pad capacity at VP_04 slot 7: no override, capacity 2, fine. VP_02 pad capacity: arrivals and departures. Mission_04 arrival at VP_02 slot 9. Mission_02 departs at 10 from VP_02. That uses pad? Departure at slot 10: pad needed. Arrival at slot 9 also pad. Pad capacity is 2 default. So no conflict. Also check corridor capacities: PATH_06 uses COR_06, PATH_04 uses COR_04. COR_04 not closed? No closures on COR_04. So ok.\n\nBut there is an issue: Mission_02 release slot 9, but we depart at 10. Release slot is the earliest departure. 10 is after release, so allowed. However, is the due slot 12? Arrival with travel 1 slot is 11, okay.\n\nNow, what about Mission_05? UAV_02 after Mission_02 arrival at VP_01 slot 11, turnaround 1 slot, so free at slot 12. But Mission_05 release is 17, so we must wait. Depart at 17, arrival 18 (travel 1). Due 26, fine. So schedule: Mission_05 depart 17. No charging needed, but we might charge while waiting? That would increase SOC, but we don't need. However, charging could increase \"energy\" metric (maybe total energy used? Or lower energy? Probably we want to minimize energy consumption? The objective \"energy\" might be total energy consumed in kWh? Or maybe weighted charge? The task says: \"Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" So we want to minimize urgent holds, then total holds, then repair distance, then weighted tardiness, then delay plus charge wait, then energy. So energy is last, so we don't care much, but we should minimize it if all else equal. So 0 charge blocks minimizes additional energy? Actually charging adds energy to battery, not consumed energy? Probably energy metric is something like total energy used across flights. Path choices affect energy. We are already using shortest energy paths? Not necessarily. For Mission_01, PATH_02 energy 0.1017, PATH_09 0.113, PATH_08 0.1356. PATH_02 is lowest energy and works. For Mission_02, PATH_04 energy 0.160815, vs PATH_01 0.302, PATH_07 0.151 but closed. PATH_07 lower but closed, so can't. PATH_04 is lower than PATH_01. So we pick lowest feasible. For Mission_03 only PATH_05. Mission_04 only PATH_06. Mission_05 only PATH_03. So paths are fixed essentially. So we can't affect energy much. Charging doesn't directly affect energy metric probably.\n\nBut there may be a hidden catch: the initial SOC override for UAV_01 reduces energy significantly, so we had to charge. For UAV_02, no override, so energy is fine.\n\nNow, let's double-check all constraints, including incoming deadhead: all missions have incoming_deadhead null, so no deadhead to consider. The order of events: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So our flow:\n\nFor UAV_01 Mission_01: depart 6, travel 2, arrival 8. During service flight, energy deducted. At arrival (end of slot 8), reserve check. Then destination turnaround at VP_02: turnaround 1 slot, so occupies slot 9? The turnaround slot is a slot after arrival. Typically, if arrival at end of slot 8, turnaround takes slot 9? Actually slots are discrete time units. Departure at start of slot d, travel occupies slots d, d+1,... d+travel_slots-1, and arrival occurs at the end of slot d+travel_slots-1, so the UAV becomes available at the next slot after turning? The turnaround_slots after arrival: so after arrival, it must undergo turnaround for specified slots before it can be used for next mission. So if arrival at end of slot 8, then turnaround slot 9, then it can depart at slot 10? But in our case, Mission_01 arrival 8, turnaround 1 -> earliest depart for next mission is 10? But we set Mission_03 depart 9. That might be wrong. Let's check the typical interpretation: In these tasks, depart_slot is the slot at which the UAV begins its service flight. The travel_slots is the duration. So if depart 6, travel 2, it occupies slots 6 and 7 (if travel_slots=2, offsets 0, then it occupies slots 6 and 7? Actually the edge_segments have offset_slot 0, duration_slots 2. So it occupies slot 6 (offset 0) and slot 7 (offset 1). Arrival after slot 7? Or after slot 8? I think travel_slots includes the departure slot? For example, travel_slots=2, depart 6, arrival at end of slot 7? The baseline for Mission_01 had depart 6, path_id PATH_08 travel_slots 2, and due 8. Arrival at 8? Actually baseline had depart 6, and due 8, so arrival must be <=8. So with travel 2, arrival = depart + travel_slots = 8. So that would be if travel slots occupy depart slot and one more, arrival at end of segment is depart + travel_slots. So travel 2 from 6 arrives at 8. So the turnaround slot is the next slot after arrival? The normative event order: after service flight, reserve check, then destination turnaround. The turnaround consumes turnaround_slots. So if arrival at slot 8, then turnaround takes slot 9? Then next departure can be slot 10. But Mission_03 depart set to 9, which conflicts. In baseline, Mission_01 dep 6, travel 2, arr 8, Mission_03 dep 9. That implies turnaround may be included within the arrival slot? Or maybe the turnaround_slots is number of slots needed after arrival, but depart can be as early as arrival+1? Let's check baseline decisions for UAV_01: MISSION_01 depart 6, PATH_08 travel 2, arrival 8. MISSION_03 depart 9. So turnaround 1 slot, yet they depart at 9. That suggests that a 1-slot turnaround means the UAV is ready to depart at slot 9, i.e., no extra slot after arrival. Or turnaround slot is the same as arrival slot? Possibly the turnaround_slots is performed after arrival but before the UAV can be used, but the turnaround slot is considered to be the same as the last travel slot? Actually, typical handling: departure slot is the start of the mission flight. The UAV arrives at the destination at the end of the travel duration, i.e., at slot depart + travel_slots - 1? If travel_slots=2, depart 6, then the flight occupies slots 6 and 7, and UAV is available after slot 7 plus turnaround. So with turnaround 1, it would be available at slot 8? Then next depart could be 8. But baseline shows next depart 9. So maybe turnaround 1 means the UAV is busy for 1 full slot after the travel slots. So if arrival at end of slot 7 (after slot 7 activities), turnaround occupies entire slot 8, then earliest departure slot 9. So that would mean arrival slot = depart + travel_slots - 1, then turnaround slot = next slot, so earliest next depart = depart + travel_slots + 1. In baseline, depart 6, travel 2, turnaround 1, next depart 9 => 6+2+1 =9. So that matches. So arrival slot is considered the end of the last travel slot. So we need to compute availability for next mission as previous depart + previous travel_slots + turnaround_slots. So for Mission_01 depart 6, travel 2, turnaround 1 => next possible depart = 6+2+1 = 9. That works. So Mission_03 depart 9 is valid.\n\nNow for UAV_02: Mission_04 depart 7, travel 2, turnaround 1 => next depart = 7+2+1 = 10. So Mission_02 can depart at 10, ok. Mission_02 depart 10, travel 1, turnaround 1 => next possible depart = 10+1+1 = 12. So after Mission_02, UAV_02 could depart at 12. But Mission_05 release is 17, so we delay. So depart 17 works. So all good.\n\nNow check pad capacity for departures: VP_02, Mission_03 departs at 9. So pad VP_02 at slot 9: departures: Mission_03 (UAV_01) and also arrival from Mission_04? Mission_04 arrival at VP_02 is at slot 7+2-1? Arrival slot = depart + travel_slots - 1 = 7+2-1=8? Or slot 9? Let's compute arrival end: if depart 7, travel slots 2 (slots 7 and 8), arrival at end of slot 8? Or end of slot 8? Then turnaround slot 9, so UAV_02 busy until slot 9. But pad usage for arrival might be at slot 8? And departure at 10? But Mission_03 departure at 9 from VP_02. So at slot 9, VP_02 has a departure (UAV_01) and possibly UAV_02 turning around? Turnaround likely uses the vertiport pad or not? Pad capacity might be needed for departures and arrivals. Usually pad capacity applies to each usage (arrival, departure, turnaround?). The instance says \"pad_capacity_by_slot_default\" with values 2. So we need to ensure at each slot, the number of UAVs using the vertiport's pad (simultaneous arrivals, departures, and maybe turnaround) does not exceed pad capacity. In our schedule:\n- VP_04 slot 6: departure UAV_01 (1), pad capacity 1, ok.\n- VP_04 slot 7: departure UAV_02 (1), capacity 2, ok.\n- VP_02 slot 8: arrival UAV_01? Arrival at end of slot 7? Actually arrival occurs at end of travel: depart 6, travel 2, arrival at end of slot 7. So arrival occupies pad at slot 7? Or slot 8? Typically, arrival pad usage aligns with the arrival slot, which is at the end of the travel. For Mission_01, travel slots 6 and 7, arrival at end of 7. So pad usage at VP_02 for arrival would be slot 7 or 8? In many formulations, arrivals consume a landing slot at the vertiport at the time they arrive, which is slot + travel_slots - 1? We'll adopt that the arrival occurs at slot depart+travel_slots-1, and that slot is when the UAV is landing and occupying pad. Then turnaround might occupy the next slot(s). But we need to be explicit: The instance doesn't specify exact slot usage for pad. But we can infer from baseline that they had no conflicts because pad capacity was 2, so even if both UAVs use same slot, it's fine. Here, VP_04 slot 6 capacity 1, so we need to avoid conflict. With Mission_01 departure at slot 6, that's 1 departure, uses pad at slot 6? Yes. So if Mission_04 also departs at 6, that would be two departures, exceeding cap 1. So we set Mission_04 depart 7, which uses pad at slot 7. At slot 7, capacity default 2, ok.\n\nNow VP_02: At slot 7, Mission_01 arriving? Arrival at end of slot 7: pad usage at VP_02 at slot 7? Possibly. But pad capacity VP_02 is 2, no closures. So that's fine. Slot 8: UAV_01 turnaround? Turnaround likely uses pad? Possibly, but capacity 2, so no problem. Slot 9: UAV_01 departure (Mission_03) and UAV_02 turnaround? Turnaround slot 9 for UAV_02 (since arrived at 8, turnaround 1 slot 9). So pad usage at slot 9: one departure and one turnaround. That's 2, equal to capacity, ok. Slot 10: UAV_02 departure (Mission_02). VP_02 pad at 10: departure 1, ok. So no pad capacity issues.\n\nNow charger capacity: We need to assign charge_blocks. For UAV_01, we charge 1 block after Mission_01 at VP_02. Charging occurs after turnaround, so post-service charge for Mission_01 occurs after slot 9? Mission_01 arrival 7, turnaround 8, post-service charge would start at slot 9? According to normative order: after turnar